      "title": "An elevator, a brick wall and a bathroom: scientists use gravity to send ‘practically unblockable’ message",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/nov/20/scientists-communicate-using-gravity-elevator-brick-wall-bathroom-experiment",
      "description": "<p>Australian physicists chose to transmit the word ‘gravity’ in binary code through brick wall</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Australian scientists have used gravity as a means of communication, in a proof-of-concept experiment involving a snail-paced 1950s elevator, a brick wall and a men’s bathroom.</p><p>Physicists at the University of Queensland have used gravity to transmit a “practically unblockable” message about 70cm through a brick wall, by sending an elevator up and down between two floors and measuring changes in the gravitational field as a result of the movement.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/nov/20/scientists-communicate-using-gravity-elevator-brick-wall-bathroom-experiment\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-19T23:30:26"
    }
